-- title:
--   Integral closedness of R'⊗_R A from reduced special fibre
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $F$ a field equipped with an $R$-algebra structure, and $A$ an $R$-subalgebra of $F$ whose underlying $R$-module is flat. Let $R'$ be a commutative $R$-algebra and $\varpi \in R'$ an element which is a non-zero-divisor. Let $K'$ be a commutative $R'$-algebra realising the localisation of $R'$ away from $\varpi$, i.e. $K' = R'[1/\varpi]$, and also an $R$-algebra compatibly with $R \to R' \to K'$, and assume that the $K'$-algebra $K' \otimes_R A$ is a domain and is integrally closed in its fraction field. Let $k'$ be a commutative $R'$-algebra, again an $R$-algebra compatibly with $R \to R' \to k'$, such that the structure map $R' \to k'$ is surjective with kernel the ideal generated by $\varpi$; assume the ring $k' \otimes_R A$ is reduced. Then $R' \otimes_R A$ is a domain and is integrally closed. (The flatness of $A$ is the flatness of the subalgebra's carrier as an $R$-module; no further hypothesis relating $A$ to $F$ is used beyond $A$ being a subalgebra of a field.)
--
--   This is the standard statement that, over a base with one non-zero-divisor $\varpi$ inverted, normality of a flat algebra can be checked on the generic fibre together with reducedness of the special fibre — the usual criterion for normalisation to commute with base change to a discrete valuation ring. It is the form in which the criterion is applied to chart rings of normal models of modular curves, which arise as $R$-subalgebras of a function field; it is cited in the construction of integral models for the Igusa scheme and in the two-chart integral model of $X_0(N)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Subalgebra_isDomain_and_isIntegrallyClosed_tensor_of_isReduced_fibre.lean

